// ==========================================================
// ASSIGNMENT 8
// SECURE FILE SHARING BETWEEN CLOUD INSTANCES
// ==========================================================


// ==========================================================
// AIM
// ==========================================================

// Create a cloud-based environment where two virtual
// machines communicate securely within a virtual network.

// Transfer files securely between the instances while
// ensuring proper access permissions.


// ==========================================================
// PROBLEM FACED
// ==========================================================

// AWS Student / Free Tier account had:
//
// vCPU Limit = 1
//
// A t2.micro instance uses 1 vCPU.
//
// Therefore:
// Two EC2 instances could not run simultaneously.
//
// Error:
//
// "You have requested more vCPU capacity than your
// current vCPU limit allows"


// ==========================================================
// SOLUTION USED
// ==========================================================

// Instead of using two simultaneously running EC2 instances,
// we simulated:
//
// VM1 (Sender)
// ↓ SCP Secure Transfer
// VM2 (Receiver)
//
// using two folders inside a single EC2 instance.
//
// This still demonstrates:
//
// 1. SCP Secure Transfer
// 2. SSH Concepts
// 3. Linux Permissions
// 4. Cloud Environment
// 5. Secure File Sharing


// ==========================================================
// TECHNOLOGIES USED
// ==========================================================

// AWS EC2
// SSH
// SCP
// Linux Permissions
// Ubuntu


// ==========================================================
// STEP 0
// CONNECT TO EC2 INSTANCE
// ==========================================================

/*

cd ~/Downloads

chmod 400 lp2.pem

ssh -i lp2.pem ubuntu@YOUR_PUBLIC_IP

*/


// ==========================================================
// STEP 1
// CREATE TWO VIRTUAL MACHINE FOLDERS
// ==========================================================

/*

mkdir VM1

mkdir VM2

*/


// ==========================================================
// STEP 2
// VERIFY FOLDERS
// ==========================================================

/*

ls

*/


// Expected Output:

// VM1
// VM2


// ==========================================================
// STEP 3
// CREATE SAMPLE FILE IN VM1
// ==========================================================

/*

echo "Secure File Sharing Assignment" > VM1/sample.txt

*/


// ==========================================================
// STEP 4
// VERIFY FILE IN VM1
// ==========================================================

/*

ls VM1

*/


// Expected Output:

// sample.txt


// ==========================================================
// STEP 5
// SECURE FILE TRANSFER USING SCP
// ==========================================================

/*

scp VM1/sample.txt VM2/

*/


// WHAT THIS DOES:

// VM1 → Sender Virtual Machine
// VM2 → Receiver Virtual Machine
//
// SCP securely transfers the file between them.


// ==========================================================
// STEP 6
// VERIFY FILE RECEIVED IN VM2
// ==========================================================

/*

ls VM2

*/


// Expected Output:

// sample.txt


// ==========================================================
// STEP 7
// OPEN FILE IN VM2
// ==========================================================

/*

cat VM2/sample.txt

*/


// Expected Output:

// Secure File Sharing Assignment


// ==========================================================
// STEP 8
// APPLY SECURE FILE PERMISSIONS
// ==========================================================

/*

chmod 400 VM2/sample.txt

*/


// PURPOSE:
//
// Restricts access permissions.
// Only owner can read the file.


// ==========================================================
// STEP 9
// VERIFY FILE PERMISSIONS
// ==========================================================

/*

ls -l VM2

*/


// Expected Output:

// -r-------- sample.txt


